The ultimate brunch entertaining and holiday morning shortcut. Tender diced ham, scallions, and two kinds of melted cheese are suspended in a velvety egg custard inside a flaky pie crust. You can assemble this quiche days or weeks ahead, freeze it raw, and pop it directly into the oven without thawing for a fresh-baked breakfast on demand.

*Adapted from [Let's Dish Recipes](https://www.letsdishrecipes.com/2013/03/make-ahead-quiche.html).*

## Ingredients

- 1 unbaked 9-inch deep-dish pie crust (store-bought refrigerated/frozen, or homemade)
- 1 cup shredded sharp cheddar cheese
- 1 cup shredded Monterey Jack cheese (or Swiss or Gruyère)
- 1 tablespoon all-purpose flour
- ½ cup cooked ham, diced small (or cooked crumbled bacon)
- 4 large eggs
- 1 cup half-and-half (or ½ cup heavy cream + ½ cup whole milk)
- 2 green onions (scallions), thinly sliced
- ¼ teaspoon kosher salt
- ¼ teaspoon dry ground mustard
- 1 pinch freshly cracked black pepper

## Instructions

1. **Prep the Cheese Barrier:** In a medium bowl, toss the shredded cheddar and Monterey Jack cheeses with 1 tablespoon of all-purpose flour. This light dusting of starch prevents the cheese from weeping oils and helps thicken the custard against the bottom crust, preventing the dreaded soggy bottom.
2. **Layer the Fillings:** Spread the floured cheese evenly across the bottom of the raw 9-inch pie crust. Scatter the diced cooked ham over the cheese.
3. **Whisk the Custard:** In a separate medium bowl, whisk together the 4 eggs, half-and-half, kosher salt, dry mustard, black pepper, and sliced green onions until the mixture is uniform and lightly frothy.
4. **Pour into Crust:** Slowly pour the seasoned egg custard over the ham and cheese in the pie crust. The liquid will seep down through the crevices.
5. **Method A — Make-Ahead Freezer Storage (Recommended):**
   - Keep the quiche on a flat level surface. Cover the surface tightly with plastic wrap to prevent freezer burn, then wrap securely in heavy-duty aluminum foil, crimping the edges around the pie pan.
   - Freeze flat in the freezer for up to 2 months.
   - **Baking from Frozen:** When ready to enjoy, **do not thaw!** Preheat your oven to 400°F (200°C). Remove the foil and plastic wrap. Loosely fold strips of aluminum foil around the fluted crust edges to prevent them from over-browning. Place the rock-frozen quiche on a baking sheet and bake for 60 to 70 minutes, until the center is puffed, a knife inserted in the center comes out clean, and the top is golden brown.
6. **Method B — Baking Fresh Immediately:**
   - Preheat oven to 350°F (175°C). Place the freshly assembled quiche on a rimmed baking sheet and bake for 45 to 55 minutes until the custard has set and the crust is golden brown.
7. **Rest and Slice:** Let the quiche rest on the counter for 10 minutes before slicing. This brief rest lets the custard set cleanly so slices hold their shape.

## Notes

- **Why Bake from Frozen?** Baking straight from frozen prevents the pastry crust from getting soggy while thawing, ensuring a crisp, flaky bottom crust.
- **Vegetarian Option:** Swap the diced ham for ½ cup of sautéed spinach (squeezed dry), sliced mushrooms, or chopped roasted bell peppers.
